<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      FS.GG.Templates#380. `RestoreLockedMode` above pins each package's identity,
      version and CONTENT HASH — so it is only enforceable if exactly one archive can
      ever answer for a given id+version. For FSharp.Core that is not true by default.

      The F# SDK appends its own bundled folder feed to the restore sources:

        sdk/<ver>/FSharp/Microsoft.FSharp.NetSdk.targets:69
          <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder)' != 'true' ">
            <RestoreAdditionalProjectSources>...;$(_FSharpCoreLibraryPacksFolder)</...>

      That folder ships FSharp.Core at the SDK's own version — the same 10.1.302 this
      workspace resolves — but as a DIFFERENT ARCHIVE from the one on nuget.org. Two
      byte-different packages, one id and version, both reachable in the same restore:

        nuget.org      contentHash bN7XMQrAQg1dyHScJ7FcsznERBkvm74LtIo1O6C5mUzg...
        library-packs  contentHash wFeXgAqIWoV1Hr++7HjtlmKGA/ft9/1XAJIvOtDNDYGu...

      Which one gets recorded is not a property of this project: a single-project
      restore into a cold cache resolved nuget.org, while a solution restore into the
      same cold cache resolved library-packs. One build performs several restores, so
      two of them can disagree — and locked mode reports that disagreement as

        error NU1403: Package content hash validation failed for FSharp.Core.10.1.302.
                      The package is different than the last restore.

      which is #380 exactly. `<clear />` in NuGet.config cannot close this: the folder
      arrives through RestoreAdditionalProjectSources (MSBuild), not through NuGet
      configuration, so nothing in NuGet.config removes it. This property is the lever
      that does. With it set, FSharp.Core resolves only from the source NuGet.config
      names, on every machine, so the committed hash beside it is achievable rather
      than a coin flip.

      Cost of setting it: FSharp.Core is fetched from nuget.org like every other
      package this workspace uses, instead of from the SDK's offline copy. This
      workspace already requires nuget.org for xunit, Thoth and the Fable packages,
      so it forfeits no offline capability it otherwise had.
    -->
    <DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder>true</DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
